          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"Goneril\",\"addressee\":\"Lear\",\"anchor_quote\":\"Sir, I love you more than word can wield the matter;\\nDearer than eyesight, space, and liberty;\\nBeyond what can be valu’d, rich or rare;\\nNo less than life, with grace, health, beauty, honour;\\nAs much as child e’er lov’d, or father found;\\nA love that makes breath poor and speech unable;\\nBeyond all manner of so much I love you.\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"The focal expression directly presents Goneril's loving emotional state toward Lear as true. The rest of her turn intensifies that avowal through comparisons claiming unsurpassable filial love.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love you more than word can wield the matter;\\nDearer than eyesight, space, and liberty;\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.92,\"reason\":\"Neither the focal words nor Goneril's elaboration position her as undertaking, renewing, or invoking a commitment or obligation. She claims the magnitude of her present love in response to Lear's test; she does not promise future conduct or affirm herself as bound by a filial duty. Lear's announced distribution of property is his own undertaking and the incentive for her declaration, not a commitment made by Goneril through this occurrence.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Which of you shall we say doth love us most?\\nThat we our largest bounty may extend\\nWhere nature doth with merit challenge.—Goneril,\\nOur eldest born, speak first.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.91,\"reason\":\"The expression occurs within a sustained, formally structured answer to a public solicitation. Its elaborate comparative rhetoric supports an intentional avowal rather than a reflex-like affective discharge; intensity alone does not establish E.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Sir, I love you more than word can wield the matter;\\nDearer than eyesight, space, and liberty;\\nBeyond what can be valu’d, rich or rare;\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":3,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.94,\"reason\":\"Beyond avowing love, the expression functions as a competitive answer to Lear's love test and as a persuasive claim to filial merit. Lear immediately accepts that answer for purposes of allocating the first portion of the kingdom, so this courtly, reward-directed function is substantial even though it is not itself a promise.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Which of you shall we say doth love us most?\\nThat we our largest bounty may extend\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Of all these bounds, even from this line to this,\\nWith shadowy forests and with champains rich’d,\\nWith plenteous rivers and wide-skirted meads,\\nWe make thee lady: to thine and Albany’s issue\\nBe this perpetual.\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"In a public court setting, Lear asks his daughters to say which loves him most and explicitly links their answers to the size of their allotted inheritance. Goneril answers first with the focal avowal and an extended claim of incomparable filial love.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"mixed\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"The focal occurrence does not establish an undertaking by Goneril. Lear nevertheless treats her declaration as a successful answer to his test and grants her and Albany's issue a perpetual territorial share. That is uptake of her avowal and competitive claim, not acceptance of a promise. Her explicit claim supplies evidence of affection, while its strategically rewarded setting, extreme hyperbole, and Cordelia's contrasting asides leave sincerity unsettled.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied title and text identify the work as William Shakespeare's The Tragedy of King Lear, which I recognise independently.\",\"limitations\":\"The supplied scene supports classification of the utterance's public force but does not settle Goneril's sincerity, private emotional state, or subsequent conduct. Cordelia's asides create grounds for doubt but do not conclusively establish that Goneril feels no affection.\"}"
